Command Pattern
The Command Pattern is a behavioral design pattern.
It encapsulates a request as an object, allowing you to parameterize clients with different requests, queue or log requests, and support undoable operations.

Components:
    Command interface: declares an execute method.
    ConcreteCommand: implements the command interface, binding an action and its receiver.
    Receiver: knows how to perform the work.
    Invoker: asks the command to execute the request.
    Client: creates and configures commands.

Real Use Case: GUI Button Actions (Undo/Redo in Text Editor)
    Each button click (e.g., Copy, Paste, Undo) is encapsulated as a command object.
    The invoker (button) calls execute on the command.
    The receiver (editor) performs the actual operation.

Pseudocode Plan
    Implement a simple remote control using the Command Pattern.
    There is a Light (Receiver) with on/off methods.
    Define a Command interface with an execute() method.
    Create two concrete commands: LightOnCommand and LightOffCommand.
    The RemoteControl (Invoker) can set and execute commands.

// Java
package com.example.command;

// Command interface
public interface Command {

    void execute();
}

// Java
package com.example.command;

// Receiver
public class Light {

    public void on() {
        System.out.println("Light is ON");
    }

    public void off() {
        System.out.println("Light is OFF");
    }
}


// Java
package com.example.command;

// Concrete Command
public class LightOnCommand implements Command {

    private final Light light;

    public LightOnCommand(Light light) {
        this.light = light;
    }

    @Override
    public void execute() {
        light.on();
    }
}


// Java
package com.example.command;

// Concrete Command
public class LightOffCommand implements Command {

    private final Light light;

    public LightOffCommand(Light light) {
        this.light = light;
    }

    @Override
    public void execute() {
        light.off();
    }
}

// Java
package com.example.command;

// Invoker
public class RemoteControl {

    private Command command;

    public void setCommand(Command command) {
        this.command = command;
    }

    public void pressButton() {
        command.execute();
    }
}


// Java
package com.example.command;

// Client
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Light light = new Light();

        Command lightOn  = new LightOnCommand(light);
        Command lightOff = new LightOffCommand(light);

        RemoteControl remote = new RemoteControl();

        remote.setCommand(lightOn);
        remote.pressButton();   // Output: Light is ON

        remote.setCommand(lightOff);
        remote.pressButton();   // Output: Light is OFF
    }
}

